<Layout
  title={"Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere: Where Exact Replay Ends and Tolerance Begins"}
  description={"About: Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere. Executed multi-ISA GE-H1 (0/20,500 SHA-256 mismatches across 25 cells on arm64 macOS, aarch64 Linux, and AMD/Intel x86_64 Linux), cross-ISA GE-H2 (t_div = 10 Apple libm vs GNU glibc sin/cos, t_div = 406 x86_64 FMA vs arm64 fmadd), and cross-GPU GE-H3 wgpu Metal/Vulkan tolerance (PSNR 64.2–72.1 dB, SSIM >= 0.99983, max pixel diff <= 2/255)."}
>
  <section class="px-6 md:px-10 pt-16 md:pt-24 pb-20 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <p class="section-label mb-4">Research study · about</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-brand-white tracking-tight">
      Writing a Game Once and Running It Everywhere: Where Exact Replay Ends and Tolerance Begins
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-body text-base text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
      This study asks whether a game can be written once as a checked state machine and then projected across languages, CPU instruction set architectures, and GPU rendering stacks. Both the surveyed specifications and three executed falsifier suites (Section 6) establish the boundary: <strong>yes</strong> for game rules and simulation under pinned integer arithmetic (<code>GE-H1</code>: <code>0 / 20,500</code> <code>SHA-256</code> checkpoint mismatches across <code>25</code> target × host cells spanning <code>arm64</code> macOS, <code>aarch64</code> Linux, and AMD/Intel <code>x86_64</code> Linux); <strong>no</strong> when standard library transcendentals or default fused multiply-add contraction cross OS/ISA boundaries in chaotic collisions (<code>GE-H2</code>); and <strong>no</strong> for bit-exact pixels across graphics hardware, yet <strong>yes</strong> under a checked perceptual tolerance gate (<code>GE-H3</code> across Apple <code>Metal</code>, AMD <code>RADV</code> Vulkan, NVIDIA Vulkan, and Intel <code>ANV</code> Vulkan; <code>measured_j = None</code>, <code>board_metered = false</code>).
    </p>
    <div class="mt-8 glass-card p-6">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] tracking-[2px] uppercase text-brand-gray mb-3">In plain words</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 font-body text-sm text-brand-slate leading-relaxed">
        <li>Under <code>Q32.32</code> 64-bit integer physics (<code>GE-H1</code>, <code>100,000</code> ticks), a 16-body 2D arena replays bit-for-bit across native Rust, C++20 (Clang and GCC), WebAssembly, Node.js <code>BigInt</code>, and Python across Apple M5 Max, Apple M3 Ultra, NVIDIA Jetson Thor, AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395, and Intel Core i9-13900H (<code>0 / 20,500</code> <code>SHA-256</code> mismatches; <code>0.073–13.60 J</code> macOS <code>L3</code> <code>reported_j</code>; <code>1.89–181.87 J</code> Linux <code>L2</code> RAPL package <code>reported_j</code>).</li>
        <li>Under IEEE 754 floating-point physics (<code>GE-H2</code>, <code>25,000</code> ticks), even <em>without</em> <code>-ffast-math</code>, calling <code>std::sin</code>/<code>std::cos</code> across Apple <code>libSystem</code> <code>libm</code> vs GNU <code>glibc</code> diverges at tick <code>10</code> (gameplay desync at tick <code>9,798</code>), and default hardware <code>FMA</code> lowering (<code>x86_64</code> <code>VFMADD</code> vs <code>arm64</code> <code>FMADD</code>) diverges at tick <code>406</code> (gameplay desync at tick <code>11,205</code>).</li>
        <li>Across four GPU vendors on headless <code>wgpu</code> (<code>GE-H3</code>, <code>512×512</code> <code>RGBA8Unorm</code>), <code>24 / 24</code> physical GPU frame pairs differ in raw <code>SHA-256</code> (<code>1.3%–9.3%</code> pixels differ by <code>1–2</code> LSB, <code>Δ_max ≤ 2</code>) while <code>24 / 24</code> pass perceptual tolerance (<code>PSNR 64.16–72.06 dB</code>, <code>SSIM ≥ 0.99983</code>). Explore the interactive cross-GPU inspector and playable joule-budgeted <code>Q32.32</code> Chrono-Budget Arena at <a href="/living/game-engines/" class="text-brand-cyan underline">/living/game-engines/</a>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

